    "turn_text": "your host, Jamie Weinstein. Every week I interview the biggest names in politics, business and media to glean some of the lessons they learned on their various roads to success. I also get them to open up on the most important issues of the day. So come along with me. We'll have some fun and hopefully learn something while we're at it. Welcome to another episode of the Jamie Weinstein show, the first since the shocking, at least to me, election last Tuesday, where Donald J. Trump became the president elect of the United States of America, most commentators, most people in Washington thought he would lose. I was one of them. There was a time back during the primaries where I thought he could win the presidency if he pivoted in certain ways in the general election. I didn't see him do that and I thought he was doomed, like most others, except A certain interview that I did right before the election, the one that came out the Monday before the election with Bill Mitchell. Bill Mitchell turned out to be right on at least his poll analysis where there was this under Trump vote that wasn't being counted. And I think we saw a little bit of that on election night. So here we are in Trump's world or soon will be in Trump's world. So today's interview is with someone who supported Donald Trump, endorsed Donald Trump, one of the youngest, maybe the youngest Senator sitting today in the Senate, Senator of Arkansas, Tom Cotton. Now, if you know Tom Cotton, you know he has a pretty remarkable story. Tom Cotton went to Harvard, went to Harvard Law, After Harvard Law decides, feels compelled to join the US military, it's right after 9-11, joins the military, goes in the infantry, fights in Iraq, fights in Afghanistan, is a combat veteran, comes back to the state's serves in works for McKinsey and company for a little bit, before being elected to the House of Representatives. And soon after that, to the United States Senate in his mid to late 30s. Tom Cotton was considered someone who was thinking of perhaps running for president in 2020 if Donald Trump did not win. He didn't say that, but he was making all the moves to suggest that he might be a soon to be presidential candidate if Trump lost on Tuesday. Trump did not lose. So Cotton probably is not looking at a 2020 run, but he is someone who has talked to Donald Trump since the election. I asked him in this interview if He's talked to Trump about possibly joining the administration. You'll have to listen to see how he answers that. But we talk about any number of issues that I think will be of interest to you, including foreign policy, something that Tom Cotton has spent a lot of time working on in the Senate. So this interview, I think, is going to be of interest in many different ways to you, the listener. But I want to remind everyone to continue interacting with the show. Keep emailing me with your ideas for guests and future guests, questions for guests. Tweet at me at Jamie underscore Weinstein. Go to iTunes very importantly and review me. Give me a five star rating. As you know, if I get 405 star reviews by the end of the month, I will give $4,000 away to charity, $10 for each review I get up to 400 reviews. So let's try to make that happen. Get the most reviews we can get to give the most to charity. But without further ado, I don't want to hold you up any longer. I give you Mr. Tom. Hi. Senator, thanks for joining the Jamie Weinstein Show. My pleasure. It's great to be on with you. So let's start. Where were you election night? Where did you",
