    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47243.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "Donald Trump had said during the campaign that his focus on enforcement in our country would be on criminal illegal aliens, drug dealers, gang members, violent felons. And he puts that estimate at two to three million people. I don't think many Americans think it's very controversial to begin deportation proceedings for violent felons and gang members and drug dealers who shouldn't be in our country to begin with. That's an important part of Donald Trump's immigration agenda. We also campaign on building a wall. I think that's something that needs to start right away. Congress has mandated it on multiple occasions in the past, yet It's never happened, and we need to focus on other aspects of enforcement as well, like an employment verification system with actual consequences, if not used, and if an employer hires illegal immigrants, tracking visas of people who come here on a visa legally, but overstay the visa, cracking down on sanctuary cities who won't cooperate with federal law enforcement. And then an issue that's as big as levels of legal immigration. We've been letting a million immigrants in this country every year and millions more temporary workers. There's virtually no evidence that we need more unskilled or low skilled workers in this country. So we need to fundamentally rethink our legal immigration policy to refocus on not extended family but nuclear families, spouses and minor unmarried children and high skilled or ultra high skilled workers. That's a big part of Donald Trump's appeal to the working class all across the country is that we're going to get immigration under control and make it work for American citizens who haven't seen a pay raise in a long time or who haven't Ben would get a job. And that's true whether they're a first generation immigrant or whether their forefathers came over on the Mayflower.",
