    "turn_text": "unfortunately, Iran is in a stronger position now than they were two or three years ago because so much of the benefit for Iran was front-loaded. They've already received a lot of the benefit of the sanctions relief. That doesn't mean that new sanctions can't be imposed, and that doesn't mean that they won't have their effect. But as Donald Trump has said, as I would say about any adversary, you can never take any option off the table. Force is always the last option that you would prefer. You'd rather achieve our national security objectives through diplomatic, political, economic, and financial means. But it has to be part of the equation that's one of the fundamental flaws of Barack Obama's foreign policy is he has repeatedly made threats that are not credible, especially in the last three years since he erased his so-called red line in Syria. And our adversary simply don't take him seriously. And in some cases, they hold him in contempt. I don't think that will be the case with a new President Trump, and I think the Ayatollahs may get the message that we're going to take a much tougher line towards their behavior, just like they got the message when President Reagan took office, and they released all those hostages the very day he was sworn in.",
