    "turn_idx": 48,
    "turn_text": "I don't have concerns in part because I've never met Steve. I haven't read his writings. We haven't worked together. If Donald Trump wants him in the White House, though, the president puts around him those in whom he has the highest degree of trust. Every White House works differently. Job titles matter a lot less than the president's confidence, and I think it's important the president have around him those in whom he has confidence, but I can't speak personally about Steve. I've never worked with him or seen him in action, but obviously he and Kellyanne Conway and some of the other people who came on board the Trump administration in the final three months did a pretty good job of helping get Donald Trump across the finish line, so it's understandable. And then the same way that say Barack Obama wanted David Axelrod to be in his campaign, to be in his White House that Donald Trump would want some of those key campaign advisors in his White House.",
