    "turn_text": "Well, it certainly gives you a sense of perspective about what's really consequential. You know, whenever you've had lives immediately in your hand or you've had your own life at risk taking a vote that might be a little controversial in the Senate, Florida doesn't seem so consequential after all. I think That's true, though, of all walks of life. I do encourage young people when they ask me about the military to always think seriously about it, whether they want to go on to serve in public office or whether they're going to make a career on the military, whether they think they're going to go into business. It gives you experiences and training that you can't get anywhere else in the world. More concretely in the Senate, obviously, it gives me a very fine detection system on matters of defense and security about whenever things just don't seem quite right, whether that's a foreign policy overseas or a defense department program or what have you. You know, I know the way militaries work and I know the way that militaries can break down or can get led astray by bad leadership. So it gives me some first-hand experience that is valuable when it comes to making defense policy and security policy.",
